ECON-01 WAS THE WRONG PATCH AND THIS REPLACES IT

I announced ECON-01 twice: JobOutcome captures actuals, Prediction only emits
PRICE_CAD, so margin variance is uncomputable. Before writing it I queried the
production database read-only. The premise was wrong.

    QuoteRequest 16     JobOutcome    0     FunnelEvent      0
    Project       3     Prediction    0     FloorRecord      0
    Invoice       5     Payment       3     FrameworkScoring 0

JobOutcome has zero rows. Prediction has zero rows. There is nothing on
either side to compare. All three Project rows were inserted within 0.9
seconds of each other on 2026-06-20 — seed data, not jobs. A margin-variance
engine built on that is an empty dashboard, and the brief is explicit: "only
when enough clean data exists", "do NOT create fields that staff will never
populate". Retracted.

WHAT THE DATA ACTUALLY SAYS

    status      count   priced   issued   converted   oldest       newest
    PENDING       14       0        0         0       2026-06-20   2026-09-11
    ACCEPTED       2       0        0         2       2026-06-20   2026-06-20

Sixteen quote requests. Four are June seed rows; twelve are real enquiries
arriving one at a time between June and two days ago. Fourteen are still
PENDING. Not one has ever been priced. Not one has ever had a quote issued.
The two ACCEPTED are the seeds.

The quote → price → issue → convert → invoice → payment pipeline is real, it
is wired, and it has never been run on a single genuine web lead. That is the
largest measurable revenue leak in this business and it is worth more than
everything left in the economics queue.

WHY THE DASHBOARD DID NOT SHOW IT

There is already a "New Leads Requiring Review" panel. It takes SIX rows
ordered `createdAt: 'desc'`.

Newest-first is right for a feed and exactly wrong for a queue of unanswered
work: the eighty-five-day-old enquiry was the last row in that list or off the
bottom of it. The panel showed a DATE, so "20 Jun" read as a fact rather than
as a problem. And six of fourteen with no total reads as six.

Three changes, all to the same panel rather than a new one: the query asks the
database for oldest first (sorting ten rows in memory is useless if the
database handed over the ten newest — a test asserts the order is in the
query), the row shows an AGE beside the date, and the header states the real
count and the oldest.

THE BANDS ARE THE SITE'S OWN PROMISE

today / due / overdue / cold are cut against "a senior estimator replies
within one business day" — the sentence this site's own estimate form
publishes. Anything past it is late by the business's own standard rather
than by a target I invented.

IT DOES NOT INVENT A PRICE

Where a lead states its square footage the indicative range comes from the
SAME published bands the visitor was shown, through the same function every
other surface uses, labelled "at published bands" rather than as a quote.
Where the area is unstated — most of the real leads — nothing is shown. An
unknown service yields no figure rather than taking the queue down, because
the lead still has to appear.

Pure functions over plain rows, no Prisma import, so the banding and the
arithmetic are tested without a database.

Nothing was written to the production database at any point; every query was
a read.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
here. parse-scan 622 files, zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean. 27
assertions.

